ParticleOS Stalwart provisioning
================================

The image owns Stalwart's database dependency. On first boot it initializes a
checksummed PostgreSQL cluster in /var/lib/pgsql, disables every TCP listener,
creates the unprivileged stalwart database role, and authenticates the
stalwart OS account through PostgreSQL peer authentication on
/run/postgresql. There is no database password or environment file.

PostgreSQL, role provisioning, and Stalwart are enabled by the OS. The
executable, allocator libraries, and WebUI run from the signed, release-pinned
service image selected at `/var/lib/particleos/stalwart/current.raw`; they are
not installed in the host `/usr`. A fresh
registry starts with `STALWART_RECOVERY_MODE=1`; the service journal contains a
random temporary `admin` credential which is valid only on the loopback-only
recovery UI. Retrieve it locally with:

    run0 journalctl -u stalwart.service

Complete application bootstrap as follows:

1. Connect through the console or an SSH tunnel to localhost:8080. Port 8080
   is intentionally never admitted by the public firewall.
2. Create the permanent administrator and configure the production hostname,
   TLS certificates, Ed25519 DKIM keys, domains, relay policy, and abuse
   controls before accepting production mail.
3. Restart Stalwart after creating the password-bearing administrator. The
   ParticleOS auto-recovery policy then selects the normal protocol profile;
   an unprovisioned registry remains in recovery mode.

The mail health gate reads Stalwart's post-migration mode marker on every boot
and after every application-image activation. In
recovery mode it validates the database and the loopback recovery WebUI. In
normal mode it performs bounded SMTP, SMTPS, IMAPS and HTTPS exchanges on IPv4
and IPv6, validates each TLS certificate, checks browser security headers, and
compares the served WebUI body with the digest generated from the exact RPM
payload. Its stable exit classes distinguish listener, TLS, certificate,
protocol, datastore and WebUI failures.

The public firewall admits only TCP 25, 443, 465, and 993. POP3, ManageSieve,
plaintext client protocols, PostgreSQL, and bootstrap TCP 8080 are closed.
Stalwart may create external connections only to SMTP TCP 25 and HTTPS TCP 443.
DNS packets are limited to loopback and therefore must pass through
systemd-resolved. Ordinary OS lookups use resolved's full validating stub.
Stalwart uses resolved's loopback-only `127.0.0.54:53` TCP proxy stub so DNS messages still
cross authenticated Cloudflare DNS-over-TLS while Stalwart validates the
preserved DNSSEC records locally for DANE. Blocked DoT or failed validation
makes mail resolution fail closed.

Boot-health verifies only the local systemd-resolved service and TCP proxy
contract. It deliberately does not query an Internet name: Cloudflare or
network outages are runtime delivery failures handled by normal mail retry
semantics and must not consume counted OS boot attempts. ParticleOS disables
`systemd-networkd-wait-online.service`; Stalwart and the counted health gate
start from local dependencies and bind their sockets even when the VPS has no
carrier or address. A unit condition also makes vendor or stale enablement of
the wait service non-blocking. Network configuration continues asynchronously.

The WebUI is a checksum-pinned payload inside the signed, dm-verity-protected
service image. Stalwart rejects every application-bundle URL except
`file:///usr/share/stalwart/webui.zip` and reloads that file on every process
start instead of consulting its mutable blob cache, so the WebUI is atomic with
the selected Stalwart image rather than with an OS A/B slot. The expected
digests are copied from that same mounted image into the private runtime
directory before startup and are checked by the health gate.

PostgreSQL continuous WAL archiving, verified base backups, retention rules and
the recovery procedure are documented in BACKUP-RECOVERY.md. PITR stays off
until an operator provisions and activates a separate encrypted backup mount.

The particleos_stalwart SELinux module confines the service to its labelled
configuration, WebUI, state, logs, runtime and private temporary files; the
PostgreSQL Unix socket; the system resolver; and the selected SMTP, IMAP and
HTTP port types. Diagnose the mail stack with:

    run0 systemctl status postgresql.service particleos-stalwart-database.service stalwart.service
    run0 systemctl start particleos-mailserver-health.service
    run0 journalctl -u particleos-mailserver-health.service
    run0 -u stalwart psql --host=/run/postgresql --dbname=stalwart --command='SELECT 1'

Independent application-image acquisition, compatibility metadata, atomic
selection, and rollback are documented in `IMAGE-UPDATES.md`.

Health exit codes are 10 listener absent, 20 TLS failure, 30 certificate
failure, 40 protocol failure, 50 datastore failure and 60 WebUI mismatch.

Changing to a remote database, adding protocols, or removing the loopback
bootstrap listener changes the appliance security and health model. Do that
only through a reviewed signed image change covering nftables, SELinux,
credentials, service ordering, health checks, and documentation.
